<!--Post: 7332--><!--Time: 1108406487-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST7332"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Joel<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Joel<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, February 14, 2005 - 10:41 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->This is my first post, but I hope you find it of some interest - <BR> <BR>In BTK’s letter of 1978 he writes: ‘You don&#39;t understand these things because your not underthe influence of factor x. The same thing that made Son of Sam, Jack The Ripper, Havery Glatman, Boston Strangler, Dr. H.H. Holmes Panty Hose Strangler of Florida, Hillside Strangler, Ted of The West Coast and many more infamous character kill.’ <BR> <BR>The idea of Factor X seemed familiar to me, and it was only recently I stumbled across something which may be its basis in the above communication.  <BR> <BR>A concept called ‘Faculty X’ is coined in ‘The Occult’ by Colin Wilson, a British writer whose books cover philosophy and the paranormal but who’s mainly known for his works on true crime.  The Occult was published in the US by Random House in 1971 &#40;ISBN: ISBN:0394465555&#41;. <BR>&#40;Details of a new edition at The Occult at Amazon.com - <a href="http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ISBN%3D034015263X/104-8700123-7091138" target=_top>http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ISBN%3D034015263 X/104-8700123-7091138</a> &#41; <BR> <BR>Wilson states: <BR> &#34;Faculty X is simply that latent power that human beings possess to reach beyond the present&#34; &#40;The Occult, Chapter 1&#41; <BR>“Think of Faculty X as simply being a kind of faculty which is natural to poets,” he said. “When Keats talked about ‘negative capability’ he meant that funny sort of way in which in one single stride you can open up to the whole universe and become completely receptive to it. . . . It’s like the difference between a person who can climb up a mountainside, leaping from rock to rock, and somebody who has to climb up with iron crampons. . . .“You can see that there is a sense in which an uneducated person is in a way better qualified to do that than a highly-educated person who tends to be so completely squashed by academicism that he finds it very difficult to do any genuinely intuitive thinking. And what we are talking about is intuition. Faculty X is intuition raised to a higher level.”   <BR>&#40;Colin Wilson interview, originally published in The Guardian newspaper UK, from <a href="http://www.stormloader.com/users/abrax7/seventy.htm" target=_top>http://www.stormloader.com/users/abrax7/seventy.ht m</a> &#41; <BR> <BR>Is this the mysterious Factor X BTK writes of? A form of evolutional thinking and ability latent in many but available to a select few? Is this what BTK believes links him with his fore-runners – ‘Son of Sam, Jack The Ripper, Havery Glatman’ et al? I think it’s fairly certain that BTK feels a sense of egotism and arrogance that manifests itself in his homicides and communiqués, like many of the killers he references in his letter.  <BR> <BR>While Wilson’s description of Faculty X does not explicitly link itself to serial killers or murder in general this concept of ‘elevated thinking’ amongst an elite few lends itself to the narcissistic and egotistical mindsets of many killers. <BR> <BR>I feel that a developing killer like BTK would look for some kind of reassurance in literature, art and music that would compliment and inform this viewpoint – which is why some believe he’s a reader of true crime.  Some killers have an interest in true crime, horror and underground literature which while not being a catalyst for their crimes may influence their commission. I think it’s likely  - with BTK’s name checking of famous killers in his letter and the Zodiac influences many &#40;including myself&#41; see in his work – that BTK has a strong knowledge of murder and serial killers through true crime literature. The ‘True Detective’ covers with their lurid shots of women bound and gagged would certainly appeal to him, and perhaps influenced his tastes. <BR> <BR>Therefore I think it’s feasible that BTK’s reference to ‘Factor X’ is formed from his reading of Colin Wilson’s ‘The Occult’. It may be the kind of title BTK would pick up and glean ideas from. Due to title’s publication in 1971 the book had been in circulation 7 years before BTK’s letter of 1978. BTK’s presentation of this notion in the alternative spelling of ‘Factor X’ may be due to his desire to obfuscate his ‘voice’ through spelling and his faking of poor grammar or a simple mis-remembrance. It’s also entirely possible that BTK’s ‘Factor X’ and Wilson’s ‘Faculty X’ have no connection whatsoever. <BR> <BR>However, if this makes sense then I would check the Libraries of Wichita &#40;and bookshops, although this would be tough&#41; to see if ‘The Occult’ was in circulation from 1971 to the time BTK became active. I’m unsure if the public could access library information regarding when it was borrowed or who by. With the relationship BTK has with a photocopier and engineering textbook in WSU, I suggest their library would be interesting to check out … This assumes he borrowed it from a library – if – he ever read it at all. <BR> <BR>This is just a theory, but one that may hold some possibility. I posted this for discussion and to see what others thought of the idea. It’s not groundbreaking, but a small thing that might shine a little more light onto the way BTK thinks and communicates. <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
