<!--Post: 7367--><!--Time: 1108426842-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST7367"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Johno<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Johno<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, February 14, 2005 - 4:20 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Joel, maybe BTK is not that cerebral. Another origin of the &#39;Factor X&#39; can be traced to a series of stories written by Wilmar Shiras and published between 1948-50 in the sci-fi magazine &#39;Astounding Science Fiction.&#39; These stories were collected as five chapters - In Hiding, Opening Doors, New Foundations, Problems and Children of the Atom in the 1953 paperback &#39;Cildren of the Atom.&#39; This book is set in the futute 1970&#39;s is believed by some to have inspired Stan Lee and Jack Kirby to come up with the The X-Men comic book concept. <BR>In issue # 1 of the 1963 Marvel Comics publication of The X-Men we learn of The X Factor in mutants. a mutant is a being who possesses a genetic structure not present in his parents. They are mutants because of the X Factor. When exposed to radiation while in the womb the genetic trigger that causes the mutation is known as the X Factor. <BR>BTK could have been a comic book and sci-fi fan and conciously or unconsiously may have come up with Factor X because of his interest in the X-Men comics and his knowledge of The X Factor. <BR>BTK&#39;s chapters in his communication could have been influenced by the science fiction magazine and comic book format of installments of chapters.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 7367-->
<!--Post: 7425--><!--Time: 1108492754-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST7425"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Joel<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Joel<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, February 15, 2005 - 10:39 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Thanks, Johno. I heard the X-Men connection before I think &#40;I&#39;m a comics fan!&#41; and it&#39;s also a very plausible explanation. BTK being a comics or sci-fi fan wouldn&#39;t surprise me. Just thought I&#39;d throw this out there to see what people thought of it. <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 7425-->
<!--Post: 7436--><!--Time: 1108495705-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST7436"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Embersage<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Embersage<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, February 15, 2005 - 11:28 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Here&#39;s a new twist for you to ponder. <BR>Factor x came from a Sci-fi book, The Secret Galactics/A.E. Van Vogt Orig. published in 1972 it was also published by DAW books as Earth Factor x, based on the short story &#34;The sound of Wild Laughter.&#34;  In the story the Earth turned upside down for a short time then back again, Genetically altered Aliens were made to look exactly like humans and they were placed in positions of power everywhere. You get the idea of the monster in your head.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
